review.raw:
VERDICT

Correct: your code iterates through the address character by character and correctly replaces every period with the three-character string "[.]" using linear time and space.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You used a clean range-based for loop to visit each character in the input string.

You checked for the target character '.' with a direct comparison.

You used push_back on a new string, avoiding expensive in-place shifting during iteration.

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode. There are no failing test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs or missed edge cases under the problem constraints (a valid IPv4 address always has exactly 3 dots).

One small inefficiency: ans starts with a capacity of 0. Repeatedly calling push_back forces ans to reallocate its internal buffer and copy elements multiple times as it grows.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(n) where n is the length of address, because the loop visits every character in the string once.

Space complexity: O(n) because a new string ans of length n + 6 is created to store and return the result.

Why my approach works:

Take address = "1.1". The loop inspects '1' and pushes '1'. Next it sees '.' and pushes '[', '.', and ']'. Then it sees '1' and pushes '1'. The final string is "1[.]1", which exactly matches the defanged format.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Regular Expressions (Regex)

Where it comes from:

Standard library approach common in Python, Java, and C++ std::regex solutions on LeetCode.

Idea:

Use a regular expression engine to find every dot and replace it with "[.]".

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <regex>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string defangIPaddr(string address) {
        return regex_replace(address, regex("\\."), "[.]");
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) to parse and substitute characters, but with huge constant factor overhead due to compiling the regex state machine.

Space Complexity:

O(n) to construct and return the replaced string.

What makes the next approach better:

Compiling regular expressions is very slow and heavy for a simple character swap. A simple character-by-character scan is much faster.

Approach 2: Character-by-Character Building (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial and standard introductory string manipulation.

Idea:

Loop through the input string. When you see a dot, append "[.]"; otherwise, append the character.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string defangIPaddr(string address) {
        string ans;
        for (char c : address) {
            if (c == '.') {
                ans += "[.]";
            } else {
                ans.push_back(c);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because we examine each character in address once.

Space Complexity:

O(n) to store the output string.

What makes the next approach better:

Repeatedly appending characters without pre-allocating memory causes dynamic heap reallocations. Pre-reserving memory avoids these reallocations.

Approach 3: Pre-Allocated String Building (Optimal Out-of-Place)

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode discussion solutions and standard C++ performance best practices.

Idea:

Every valid IPv4 address contains exactly 3 dots. Replacing each '.' (1 character) with "[.]" (3 characters) adds 2 extra characters per dot, so the defanged string always has length equal to address.size() + 6. We pre-reserve this exact capacity before pushing characters.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string defangIPaddr(string address) {
        string ans;
        ans.reserve(address.size() + 6);
        for (char c : address) {
            if (c == '.') {
                ans += "[.]";
            } else {
                ans.push_back(c);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) single pass with zero buffer reallocations.

Space Complexity:

O(n) for the output string buffer.

What makes the next approach better:

If the interviewer asks for O(1) extra auxiliary space and allows mutating the input string, we can modify the string in-place.

Approach 4: In-Place Two Pointers / Expansion (Optimal In-Place)

Where it comes from:

Classic string interview technique for replacing characters in-place (similar to URLify or Replace Spaces on GeeksforGeeks).

Idea:

Resize the original string by adding 6 extra characters to the end. Place one pointer at the end of the original text and one pointer at the end of the new expanded string. Move backwards: copy regular characters as they are, and write ']', '.', '[' whenever you encounter a dot.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string defangIPaddr(string address) {
        int originalLen = address.size();
        address.resize(originalLen + 6);
        int writeIdx = address.size() - 1;

        for (int readIdx = originalLen - 1; readIdx >= 0; readIdx--) {
            if (address[readIdx] == '.') {
                address[writeIdx--] = ']';
                address[writeIdx--] = '.';
                address[writeIdx--] = '[';
            } else {
                address[writeIdx--] = address[readIdx];
            }
        }
        return address;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because each position is read and written once.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space (modifying the input string in-place, assuming the parameter is mutable).

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 2 (Character-by-Character Building).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 3 (Pre-Allocated String Building). Mentioning ans.reserve(address.size() + 6) demonstrates that you understand how dynamic memory and vector/string buffers behave under the hood.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: Why did you add 6 when reserving capacity?

Answer: A valid IPv4 address always consists of 4 octets separated by exactly 3 dots. Replacing each single-character '.' with the three-character string "[.]" adds 2 extra characters per dot. With 3 dots, the string grows by exactly 3 * 2 = 6 characters.

Question 2: Can you do this in-place without creating a brand new return string?

Answer: Yes, by using the backwards two-pointer expansion technique (Approach 4). Resize the string to its final size, then scan from back to front so overwriting characters never destroys unread data.

Question 3: How would your solution change for an IPv6 address?

Answer: IPv6 addresses use colons (':') instead of dots, and can have up to 7 colons. We would count the number of colons first, reserve length + (colonCount * 2), and replace ':' with "[:]".

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

A valid IPv4 address always has 3 dots, meaning the final defanged string will always be exactly 6 characters longer than the original input.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Use ans.reserve() on output strings when the final length is known or easily calculated to avoid repeated memory reallocations.

Remember that string concatenation with ans += "[.]" or pushing characters backwards is cleaner than calling push_back three times in a row.

If an interviewer asks for O(1) extra space, think of the backwards two-pointer expansion pattern.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

String traversal, buffer pre-allocation, and in-place backward substitution.

THE BEST SOLUTION

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string defangIPaddr(string address) {
        string ans;
        // A valid IPv4 address has exactly 3 dots, adding 2 chars per dot (6 chars total)
        ans.reserve(address.size() + 6);

        for (char c : address) {
            if (c == '.') {
                ans += "[.]";
            } else {
                ans.push_back(c);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
